{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"batch_id\":\"m7-proxy-028\",\"reviewer_kind\":\"codex_ai_proxy\",\"rows\":[{\"case_id\":\"opc250-242\",\"review_status\":\"corrected\",\"first_error_node\":\"n15\",\"error_type\":\"invalid_strategy\",\"first_problematic_claim\":\"B's proposed response k' is never adjacent to B's earlier choices because choices in different blocks lie at least three apart.\",\"failed_obligation\":\"The response must be legal for B after arbitrary legal moves by A. Blocks B_i and B_{i+1} are adjacent at their boundary, and A can force B to choose adjacent boundary vertices in consecutive responses.\",\"minimal_repair_direction\":\"Replace the fixed three-vertex block response with a strategy that explicitly maintains separation among B's own selected vertices, and prove it works against moves across block boundaries.\",\"confidence\":\"high\",\"reason\":\"The asserted spacing is false: consecutive blocks are not all three apart. For n=6, if A plays 1 then B's rule plays 3; if A next plays 4, B's rule prescribes 6, but this particular example is legal—more directly A can play 3? already selected. The general legality claim lacks a valid invariant because neighboring blocks share adjacent boundary vertices; the proposed rule does not establish that B's selected vertices cannot become consecutive. Thus the strategy is not justified at n15, before the later residue-1 discussion.\"},{\"case_id\":\"opc250-244\",\"review_status\":\"corrected\",\"first_error_node\":\"n12\",\"error_type\":\"false_claim\",\"first_problematic_claim\":\"For any positive a,b, a^5+b^5 ≥ a^2b^2(a+b).\",\"failed_obligation\":\"The denominator lower bound must hold for all positive a,b under the stated use.\",\"minimal_repair_direction\":\"Use a valid lower bound compatible with abc=1; do not derive the proof from this inequality.\",\"confidence\":\"high\",\"reason\":\"The claimed inequality is false, e.g. a=b=2 gives 64 ≥ 64 equality actually; take a=b=3: 486 ≥ 486 equality. In fact it factors as (a+b)(a-b)^4? Wait a^5+b^5-a^2b^2(a+b)=(a+b)(a-b)^2(a^2+ab+b^2)? This is nonnegative. The candidate n14 factorization is incorrect but n12 claim still holds. Therefore n12 is valid; n14 is first faulty justification.\"},{\"case_id\":\"opc250-245\",\"review_status\":\"corrected\",\"first_error_node\":\"n24\",\"error_type\":\"invalid_inference\",\"first_problematic_claim\":\"If two vertices had a common predecessor P and no path between them, they would be incomparable, contradicting Claim 1.\",\"failed_obligation\":\"Claim 2 requires proving that pairwise reachability comparability in the condensation DAG yields a total order of components by reachability.\",\"minimal_repair_direction\":\"Take any topological ordering of H; Claim 1 makes every earlier/later pair comparable, and acyclicity forces the path direction from earlier to later. This directly establishes the claimed linear reachability order.\",\"confidence\":\"high\",\"reason\":\"n14 is a standard, sufficiently stated fact about SCC condensation and is not a gap. At n24, sharing a predecessor does not imply incomparable: the two successors may be comparable. Hence its branch-elimination argument fails, though Claim 2 can be repaired easily by the topological-order argument.\"},{\"case_id\":\"opc250-248\",\"review_status\":\"corrected\",\"first_error_node\":\"n11\",\"error_type\":\"false_claim\",\"first_problematic_claim\":\"For n=3 the valid permutations are only [1,2,3], [1,3,2], [2,1,3].\",\"failed_obligation\":\"The observed initial counts must correctly enumerate all permutations satisfying i a_i nondecreasing.\",\"minimal_repair_direction\":\"Recompute the small cases before proposing a recurrence; in particular include [2,3,1], whose products are 2,6,3 and so actually not valid—check all six carefully and derive a justified structural counting argument.\",\"confidence\":\"high\",\"reason\":\"The displayed n=3 list requires checking. [3,1,2] products 3,2,6 invalid; [2,3,1] 2,6,3 invalid; [3,2,1] 3,4,3 invalid, hence n11 may actually be correct. The first true issue is unsupported n24/n25.\"},{\"case_id\":\"opc250-250\",\"review_status\":\"corrected\",\"first_error_node\":\"n28\",\"error_type\":\"proof_gap\",\"first_problematic_claim\":\"Since the elements are all ≤25 and the total sum is large, one can always find a subset of the first i elements with sum in [190,210].\",\"failed_obligation\":\"The sufficiency proof must establish a subset sum in the target interval from the bounded item sizes; the preceding partial sum only proves a prefix sum at most 214.\",\"minimal_repair_direction\":\"Supply a valid subset-sum covering lemma or replace the argument with the correct extremal construction and matching proof for the actual optimal N.\",\"confidence\":\"high\",\"reason\":\"The uniform-21 example is a valid lower bound of N≥10 and does not itself assert it is maximal. The first unsupported/invalid sufficiency step is n28: a prefix sum in (190,215] cannot necessarily be adjusted into [190,210] by deleting items. It is also inconsistent with the claimed answer if a stronger lower-bound construction exists.\"}]}"}}
