city, the proof must establish the claimed total reachability order of the SCCs.\",\"minimal_repair_direction\":\"Use a topological ordering: for i<j, Claim 1 gives a path in one direction, and acyclicity/topological order rules out C_j reaching C_i; hence C_i reaches C_j.\",\"confidence\":\"high\",\"reason\":\"The standard SCC-condensation fact at n14 is sufficiently justified as a standard graph-theoretic property. n24 only observes that two incomparable successors of a common predecessor are impossible; n25 does not show that this observation yields the total chain required by Claim 2.\"},{\"case_id\":\"opc250-248\",\"review_status\":\"corrected\",\"first_error_node\":\"n24\",\"error_type\":\"proof_gap\",\"first_problematic_claim\":\"The two placements of n in positions n and n-1 are the only valid configurations.\",\"failed_obligation\":\"The recurrence requires proof that n cannot occur in any earlier position in a valid permutation.\",\"minimal_repair_direction\":\"Add a lemma proving that placing n before position n-1 forces a later violation of the monotonicity inequalities, then apply the two terminal-position bijections.\",\"confidence\":\"high\",\"reason\":\"The small counts through n=4 are correct, including the stated three valid n=3 permutations and five valid n=4 permutations. The recurrence's exhaustive split first appears at n24, where the essential exclusion of earlier positions of n is merely asserted.\"},{\"case_id\":\"opc250-250\",\"review_status\":\"corrected\",\"first_error_node\":\"n17\",\"error_type\":\"false_claim\",\"first_problematic_claim\":\"N=10 is sufficient for the second player regardless of the first player's list.\",\"failed_obligation\":\"The claimed universal subset-sum guarantee for the interval [190,210] must hold for every list of entries at most 25 and total at least 200.\",\"minimal_repair_direction\":\"Increase the lower-bound target using extremal counterexamples, then supply a matching subset-sum guarantee for the correc